1
00:00:00,180 --> 00:00:03,200
This exact ship has 45 minutes left to float.

2
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:09,340
Right now, over 4 ,000 people are eating dinner on board on the first night of a seven

3
00:00:09,340 --> 00:00:11,780
day cruise. Nobody has any reason to worry.

4
00:00:13,180 --> 00:00:15,160
The captain is on the phone with an old friend,

5
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:17,900
being told the water near the shore is deep enough.

6
00:00:19,340 --> 00:00:23,440
It isn't. In minutes, an officer will try to turn this ship away from the rocks.

7
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:25,460
The captain will order him not to.

8
00:00:26,460 --> 00:00:31,620
Hours later, that same captain will be given a direct order to get back on his own sinking

9
00:00:31,620 --> 00:00:33,380
ship. He will not comply.

10
00:00:34,980 --> 00:00:36,700
Netflix just told this story again.

11
00:00:37,100 --> 00:00:39,980
There's a real three year search they left out entirely.

12
00:00:41,660 --> 00:00:43,060
This is Black Box Breakdown.

13
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:48,040
What Netflix's Costa Concordia documentary left out.

14
00:00:49,100 --> 00:00:51,640
Chapter 1. The sail past nobody questioned.

15
00:00:52,660 --> 00:00:55,120
The Costa Concordia was built in Genoa,

16
00:00:55,400 --> 00:01:00,600
Italy, delivered in 2006 at a cost of roughly 450 million euros.

17
00:01:01,280 --> 00:01:06,040
At 114 ,000 tons, she was the first of an entirely new class of ship.

18
00:01:07,520 --> 00:01:08,820
Owned by Carnival Corporation,

19
00:01:09,360 --> 00:01:10,960
operated by Costa Crociare,

20
00:01:11,320 --> 00:01:15,800
she was designed to carry 3 ,780 passengers and 1 ,100 crew.

21
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:21,060
January 13th, 2012, she departs Civitavecchia,

22
00:01:21,280 --> 00:01:25,300
the port for Rome, on the first night of a seven day western Mediterranean cruise.

23
00:01:25,920 --> 00:01:29,520
3 ,206 passengers and just over 1 ,000 crew are aboard.

24
00:01:30,860 --> 00:01:32,800
The captain is Francesco Schatino.

25
00:01:33,360 --> 00:01:35,120
That evening, the ship's Matra D,

26
00:01:35,460 --> 00:01:37,080
a native of the island of Giglio,

27
00:01:37,520 --> 00:01:41,260
asks him for a favour. Sail close to the island as an unofficial salute.

28
00:01:42,580 --> 00:01:43,860
This wasn't unprecedented.

29
00:01:43,860 --> 00:01:46,080
The ship, or her sister ship,

30
00:01:46,260 --> 00:01:49,380
had done similar close salutes at Giglio four times before,

31
00:01:50,020 --> 00:01:53,260
always in summer, always arranged in advance with the port authority.

32
00:01:54,940 --> 00:01:56,920
Schatino calls a retired Costa captain,

33
00:01:57,300 --> 00:01:59,600
his own former mentor, who lives on the island,

34
00:02:00,160 --> 00:02:04,660
Mario Palombo. He tells Palombo the ship will sound its horn in tribute as it passes.

35
00:02:06,100 --> 00:02:10,980
At 9 .37 that night, the ship is holding a safety margin of half a nautical mile from

36
00:02:10,980 --> 00:02:13,720
the rocks off Giglio, doing 15 .4 knots.

37
00:02:14,360 --> 00:02:17,300
Palombo tells Schatino the water near the port is deep enough.

38
00:02:18,780 --> 00:02:19,980
The margin keeps shrinking.

39
00:02:20,480 --> 00:02:21,760
Half a mile becomes a quarter.

40
00:02:22,280 --> 00:02:26,560
A senior officer on the bridge grows concerned they're too close and orders the helmsman

41
00:02:26,560 --> 00:02:27,800
to turn away from the rocks.

42
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:34,420
Schatino overrides him. He takes command himself and orders the helmsman to hold course

43
00:02:34,420 --> 00:02:39,440
and increase speed. Around 9 .42 to 9

44
00:02:39,440 --> 00:02:42,300
.45 at roughly 15 to 16 knots,

45
00:02:42,820 --> 00:02:44,680
the ship's hull strikes a rock off Lascaux,

46
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:47,400
a small islet group barely visible in the dark.

47
00:02:48,840 --> 00:02:50,780
The gash is 53 meters long,

48
00:02:51,040 --> 00:02:53,480
tearing through five separate watertight compartments.

49
00:02:54,100 --> 00:02:56,300
The ship was designed to survive damage to two.

50
00:02:58,200 --> 00:03:01,920
Chapter two, 65 minutes. Within minutes,

51
00:03:02,220 --> 00:03:04,060
the flooding knocks out the ship's main power.

52
00:03:04,060 --> 00:03:09,660
At 9 .52, the crew tries and fails to restart the emergency generator.

53
00:03:10,300 --> 00:03:12,600
Over 4 ,000 people are now on a dark,

54
00:03:12,860 --> 00:03:16,660
drifting, sinking ship. For the next hour,

55
00:03:16,940 --> 00:03:17,940
the crew tells passengers,

56
00:03:18,360 --> 00:03:20,960
this is just an electrical problem under control,

57
00:03:21,400 --> 00:03:26,540
nothing to worry about. The official investigation later found this delay and

58
00:03:26,540 --> 00:03:30,820
the decision to downplay the danger to both passengers and shore authorities among the

59
00:03:30,820 --> 00:03:32,520
most serious failures of that night.

60
00:03:34,060 --> 00:03:37,420
The formal order to abandon ship isn't given until around 10 .50,

61
00:03:38,160 --> 00:03:43,160
65 minutes after impact. International rules require ships be evacurable within

62
00:03:43,160 --> 00:03:47,820
30. The evacuation itself takes almost seven hours.

63
00:03:48,260 --> 00:03:51,460
It isn't declared complete until 4 .46 the next morning.

64
00:03:52,820 --> 00:03:55,540
32 people die, 27 passengers,

65
00:03:55,920 --> 00:03:59,120
five crew. Roughly 64 are seriously injured.

66
00:04:00,640 --> 00:04:05,320
Hungarian ship's violinist Sandor Feher is reported to have helped frightened children

67
00:04:05,320 --> 00:04:08,880
into life jackets, then gone back to his cabin for his own violin.

68
00:04:09,440 --> 00:04:13,260
He didn't make it out. French passenger Francis Servel,

69
00:04:13,560 --> 00:04:17,020
71, is reported to have given his own life jacket to his wife.

70
00:04:17,460 --> 00:04:19,080
He drowned. She survived.

71
00:04:20,800 --> 00:04:22,200
Chapter three, get back on the ship.

72
00:04:23,740 --> 00:04:26,900
By this point, Captain Scatino has already left the ship,

73
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:30,020
while thousands of passengers and crew are still aboard.

74
00:04:31,340 --> 00:04:34,040
Italian Coast Guard Commander Gregorio de Falco,

75
00:04:34,460 --> 00:04:36,600
on shore in Livorno, gets him on the phone.

76
00:04:37,140 --> 00:04:39,360
He tells Scatino, on a recorded line,

77
00:04:40,020 --> 00:04:44,360
exactly what to do next. What you're about to hear is a recreation,

78
00:04:44,940 --> 00:04:47,180
built word for word from the real transcript,

79
00:04:47,680 --> 00:04:50,740
published and confirmed authentic within days of the disaster.

80
00:04:52,360 --> 00:04:56,620
You need to go to that bow using the pilot ladder and climb up onto that ship.

81
00:04:57,020 --> 00:04:59,580
Do you understand? It's dark.

82
00:05:00,100 --> 00:05:01,340
It's difficult to see anything.

83
00:05:01,760 --> 00:05:04,320
Other rescue boats are already coordinating down there.

84
00:05:05,440 --> 00:05:07,340
It's been an hour, you've told me the same thing.

85
00:05:07,760 --> 00:05:09,700
Now get on board, get on board,

86
00:05:09,960 --> 00:05:11,940
and tell me how many people are still there.

87
00:05:13,120 --> 00:05:14,760
You saved yourself from the sea,

88
00:05:15,220 --> 00:05:17,020
but I will make sure you go through hell for this.

89
00:05:17,440 --> 00:05:21,240
Get on board, damn it. Scatino never went back.

90
00:05:21,660 --> 00:05:24,420
That single phone call, roughly four minutes long,

91
00:05:24,600 --> 00:05:26,120
would follow him for the rest of his life.

92
00:05:27,560 --> 00:05:30,160
De Falco publicly rejected being called a hero.

93
00:05:30,780 --> 00:05:32,720
He said he was just doing his job.

94
00:05:33,760 --> 00:05:36,880
Chapter four, the man they couldn't find for three years.

95
00:05:38,460 --> 00:05:39,700
Recovering the dead took years,

96
00:05:40,180 --> 00:05:43,500
not days. Seventeen bodies were found in the first weeks.

97
00:05:44,140 --> 00:05:48,580
Eight more in February, using a mini submarine to search the flooded lifeboat deck.

98
00:05:50,020 --> 00:05:52,640
Five more, trapped between the hull and the seabed,

99
00:05:52,820 --> 00:05:54,300
weren't recoverable until March.

100
00:05:54,300 --> 00:05:59,160
Passenger Maria Grazia Trekorici wasn't identified until October of the following year,

101
00:05:59,360 --> 00:06:01,420
after the wreck was finally righted.

102
00:06:02,580 --> 00:06:03,860
One person was still missing.

103
00:06:04,480 --> 00:06:07,760
Russell Rubello, 32, an assistant waiter from India.

104
00:06:09,220 --> 00:06:11,180
He wasn't found until November 3rd,

105
00:06:11,360 --> 00:06:14,880
2014, nearly three years after the ship went down.

106
00:06:15,580 --> 00:06:19,920
Workers dismantling the wreck in Genoa found him in a crushed cabin identified by an

107
00:06:19,920 --> 00:06:24,120
ID card in his uniform. He left behind a young son.

108
00:06:24,580 --> 00:06:26,200
His brother has since called him a hero.

109
00:06:27,960 --> 00:06:30,780
Netflix's own 2026 documentary about this disaster,

110
00:06:31,160 --> 00:06:34,260
built around real black box audio and new survivor interviews,

111
00:06:34,420 --> 00:06:36,760
does not tell this part of the story at all.

112
00:06:38,180 --> 00:06:41,700
Almost three years is a long time for a family to wait for one more answer.

113
00:06:42,120 --> 00:06:44,020
It's the part of this story that got left out.

114
00:06:45,760 --> 00:06:48,460
Chapter five, the largest salvage in history.

115
00:06:49,620 --> 00:06:54,700
Removing a 114 ,000 ton wreck from a protected marine park was never going to be

116
00:06:54,700 --> 00:07:00,020
simple. It became by weight the largest maritime salvage operation ever attempted.

117
00:07:01,660 --> 00:07:04,720
Salvage Master Nick Sloan led roughly 500 engineers,

118
00:07:05,100 --> 00:07:07,720
divers and welders from 24 countries.

119
00:07:08,320 --> 00:07:13,340
They spent 16 months building an underwater platform and welding giant steel flotation

120
00:07:13,340 --> 00:07:14,920
tanks to both sides of the hull.

121
00:07:16,560 --> 00:07:20,800
September 16th, 2013. Using cables and counterweight,

122
00:07:21,180 --> 00:07:23,280
the crew rotates the entire wreck upright.

123
00:07:23,700 --> 00:07:25,520
The maneuver takes about 19 hours.

124
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:31,980
The following July, the ship is re -floated using those same steel tanks and towed roughly

125
00:07:31,980 --> 00:07:34,940
200 nautical miles to Genoa for scrapping.

126
00:07:36,420 --> 00:07:38,780
Total cost estimates vary depending on what's counted,

127
00:07:38,860 --> 00:07:40,980
but most run over a billion dollars.

128
00:07:41,440 --> 00:07:43,280
Some put the full figure closer to two.

129
00:07:44,920 --> 00:07:47,640
One salvage diver, Israel Franco Moreno,

130
00:07:48,060 --> 00:07:52,540
died during the operation from a leg injury while installing the flotation tanks.

131
00:07:52,860 --> 00:07:54,940
A separate death from the disaster itself,

132
00:07:55,300 --> 00:07:58,300
but a real one. Chapter six,

133
00:07:58,900 --> 00:08:00,760
ten years later, still counting.

134
00:08:02,580 --> 00:08:05,340
Captain Scutino was charged with 32 counts of manslaughter,

135
00:08:05,760 --> 00:08:07,880
causing the shipwreck, abandoning ship,

136
00:08:08,160 --> 00:08:10,560
and lying to authorities about how bad it was.

137
00:08:11,800 --> 00:08:16,900
He never pleaded guilty. He has consistently called himself a scapegoat and

138
00:08:16,900 --> 00:08:20,040
pointed to the helmsman's mistaken turn as the real cause.

139
00:08:21,700 --> 00:08:23,440
But the investigation found that correction,

140
00:08:23,900 --> 00:08:26,900
even if made perfectly, would not have prevented the collision.

141
00:08:27,640 --> 00:08:29,020
The ship was already too close,

142
00:08:29,380 --> 00:08:31,500
going too fast, far too late to save.

143
00:08:33,300 --> 00:08:36,620
February 11th, 2015, after a 19 -month trial,

144
00:08:37,080 --> 00:08:39,780
guilty on every count. Sixteen years,

145
00:08:40,180 --> 00:08:42,140
upheld on appeal in 2016,

146
00:08:42,580 --> 00:08:46,020
and finalised by Italy's highest court in 2017.

147
00:08:47,940 --> 00:08:50,040
Five other officers took plea deals instead,

148
00:08:50,240 --> 00:08:54,560
with sentences short enough under Italian law that none of them ever served a single

149
00:08:54,560 --> 00:08:57,580
day in prison. As of this year,

150
00:08:57,980 --> 00:08:59,720
Scutino is still in Robibia prison,

151
00:09:00,100 --> 00:09:02,740
in Rome, about ten years into his sentence,

152
00:09:03,200 --> 00:09:05,520
scheduled release around 2033.

153
00:09:06,700 --> 00:09:10,460
In 2025, he withdrew a request for early -day release,

154
00:09:10,840 --> 00:09:14,420
after failing to secure the job placement Italian law required for it.

155
00:09:15,640 --> 00:09:18,200
Gregorio De Falco, the man who ordered him back aboard,

156
00:09:18,660 --> 00:09:21,160
later spent four years as an elected Italian senator,

157
00:09:21,580 --> 00:09:25,400
then returned to the Coast Guard and appears to have retired earlier this year after

158
00:09:25,400 --> 00:09:27,140
more than three decades of service.

159
00:09:28,580 --> 00:09:30,480
The disaster did change real rules.

160
00:09:31,200 --> 00:09:33,960
Cruise ships must now run safety drills before leaving port,

161
00:09:33,960 --> 00:09:36,240
not within the first 24 hours at sea,

162
00:09:36,640 --> 00:09:40,360
the old standard that let this ship sail with hundreds of undrilled passengers.

163
00:09:42,060 --> 00:09:47,380
Chapter 7, The Real Cause It's tempting to make this one -man story.

164
00:09:48,200 --> 00:09:49,620
A captain who got too close,

165
00:09:49,700 --> 00:09:51,820
then ran, then paid for it.

166
00:09:52,980 --> 00:09:55,480
But the official investigation names a longer chain,

167
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:59,020
a tradition of unofficial close salutes nobody had ever stopped,

168
00:09:59,500 --> 00:10:02,300
a bridge crew that didn't challenge an order it should have,

169
00:10:02,300 --> 00:10:04,480
a company that has never fully agreed,

170
00:10:04,820 --> 00:10:07,520
in public, on who approved that night's detour.

171
00:10:08,940 --> 00:10:11,880
Four times before, the same manoeuvre had gone fine.

172
00:10:12,580 --> 00:10:16,620
This is what happens when a normalised shortcut finally meets the one night it doesn't

173
00:10:16,620 --> 00:10:19,860
work. 32 people are still dead.

174
00:10:20,420 --> 00:10:22,540
One of them took almost three years to find.

175
00:10:23,060 --> 00:10:25,220
The rules changed because of what happened to them,

176
00:10:25,300 --> 00:10:29,920
not before it. What stays with me isn't the radio call,

177
00:10:30,180 --> 00:10:35,320
famous as it is. It's that a family waited almost three years for

178
00:10:35,320 --> 00:10:36,760
one more piece of certainty,

179
00:10:36,960 --> 00:10:40,560
and the biggest retelling of this story yet didn't have room for it.

180
00:10:41,760 --> 00:10:44,740
Every ship still sailing tonight is carrying that same lesson,

181
00:10:45,040 --> 00:10:47,040
whether anyone on board is thinking about it or not.

182
00:10:48,780 --> 00:10:51,980
If a captain refusing to get back on his own sinking ship got to you,

183
00:10:52,260 --> 00:10:56,060
wait until you hear the real cockpit recording from the plane that flew itself for three

184
00:10:56,060 --> 00:10:59,640
hours. Subscribe to Black Box Breakdown.

185
